      'Look at the charging socket on an electric car. The socket is a port: the one place where the car lets the outside world reach part of its inside, and only in a limited way. Power goes in, status messages go out, and nobody plugs into the battery itself. The charging standard printed on the cable says what each end must offer — this pin supplies, that pin receives — and therefore which two sockets may be joined. The standard is an interface definition, and plugging one cable into one car at one charger is a connection that conforms to it. A cable with two supply ends charges nothing, whatever its label says.',
