    id: 'w10-structure',
    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'Structure: ports, conjugation, interfaces, connections, bindings and flows',
    paragraphs: [
      'Ports. A port definition defines features that can be made available through ports. It replaces SysML v1’s interface blocks. A port is a connection point through which a part definition makes some of its features available in a limited way, like a v1 proxy port (S10 sl.20). A port is not a part: it does not own the battery or the software it gives access to. Ports may have attribute and reference features. A feature with a direction (in, out or inout) is a directed feature, and directed features are always referential. Two ports are compatible for connection if they have directed features that match with inverse directions (S10 sl.20). Every port definition has an implicit conjugate that reverses its inputs and outputs, written with ~: port chargeInlet : ~ChargingPort (S10 sl.21). The reference model’s convention is to write each port definition from the producing side and to type the consuming side’s port with ~.',
      'Connections and interfaces. A connection definition is a part definition whose usages are connections between its ends. A connection is a usage that connects two other features, and dot notation, a feature chain such as \'PHY-003\'.chargerOutlet, reaches a nested feature in context (S10 sl.18–19). An interface definition is a connection definition whose ends are ports. An interface usage is a connection usage defined by an interface definition, and it connects two compatible ports (S10 sl.22). Multiplicities written on the ends are cross multiplicities: they constrain how many connections each participant has, not how many participants there are. The ends themselves always have multiplicity 1..1 relative to the connection definition, and the default cross multiplicity of an interface is 0..* (S10 sl.18, sl.22–23).',
      'Bindings and flows. A binding connection asserts that two features are equivalent, that is, have equal values in the same context. The feature value notation, attribute :>> chargeTimeToDispatch = 18 [min], combines a feature with a binding: it is a binding, not an initial or default value (S10 sl.24–25). A flow transfers a payload from an output of a source to an input of a target. It is actually a connection between the source and target ports (S10 sl.26–27). Flows can be defined inside an interface definition, and they are established whenever the interface is used (S10 sl.28).',
      'Logical and physical structure (course convention). SysML v2 has no separate “logical” and “physical” kinds of part. The distinction is the course’s viewpoint convention from Week 8 (RB-7.1), and it is realised by packages. Logical holds the FN actions and the LOG and AI parts that perform them. Physical holds the item defs, port defs, interface defs and PHY parts, and the IF interface usages between PHY ports. Keeping them apart is what makes allocation meaningful: you can reallocate a function without deleting it (Week 9, q9-11).',
    ],
