      'L10 and CP4 ask for a satisfy for every REQ-SYS “or a recorded gap”. A gap is not a failure of the lab. It is information the model must carry. There are two common gaps, and the listing aerolink_satisfy_gap.sysml shows both in a what-if variant of the reference design. In the first, the candidate satisfier exists but the evidence is against it. The charger supplier’s estimate has risen to 24 min, the Week 9 what-if. Writing satisfy \'REQ-SYS-001\' by PHY-003 would assert that 24 ≤ 20. The checker would accept it, but the model would contradict itself. In the second, no candidate exists: this team’s CP3 architecture has no fire-detection element, so nothing in the model conforms to REQ-SYS-009’s subject.',
      'The course convention (Week 10, adopted by the course and not prescribed by the SysML texts) is to record a gap in three places. In the model: no satisfy for that requirement, and an @Issue annotation in the requirement whose text starts “SATISFY GAP” and names the candidate, the evidence, the action and the owner, with @StatusInfo set to open. Both are standard-library metadata (ModelingMetadata); the reference model already uses @StatusInfo for its TBDs. In the L10 gap table: one row per gap with the same content. And in the AROU register, when the gap is a risk, as here with R-001. The checker accepted the listing with no errors and no warnings.',
